1 NOTE {geni:about_me} - http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piero_Strozzi
2 CONT
2 CONT Piero (or Pietro) Strozzi (c. 1510 – 21 June 1558) was an Italian military leader. He was a member of the rich Florentine family of the Strozzi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Piero Strozzi was the son of Filippo Strozzi the Younger and Clarice de' Medici.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although in 1539 he married another Medici, Laudomia di Pierfrancesco, he was a fierce opponent of the main line of that family. He fought in the army led by his father and other Florentine exile from France to oust the Medici fro
2 CONC m Florence, but, after their defeat at the Battle of Montemurlo, Piero fled to France at the court of Catherine de' Medici.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was in French service during the Italian War of 1542. Having raised an army of Italian mercenaries, he was confronted by the Spanish-Imperial forces at the Battle of Serravalle, where he was defeated. In 1548 he was in Scotland
2 CONC  supporting Mary of Guise of behalf of Henry II of France, during the war of the Rough Wooing. There he designed fortifications against the English at Leith and Haddington. As he was shot in the thigh by an arquebus at Haddington,
2 CONC  Strozzi supervised the works at Leith from a chair carried by four workmen. Strozzi also designed works at Dunbar Castle with the assistance of Migiliorino Ubaldini.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1551 he successfully defended Mirandola against the papal troops. He was named marshal of France in 1554.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later he fought in the defence of the Republic of Siena against Cosimo de' Medici, leading a French army. He obtained a pyrrhic victory at Pontedera on 11 June 1554, but his army could not receive help from the ships of his brothe
2 CONC r Leone (who had been killed by an arquebus shot near Castiglione della Pescaia) and he was forced to retreat to Pistoia. On 2 August his defeat at the Battle of Marciano meant the end of the Senese independence.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1556 he was appointed as superintendent of the Papal army and lord of Épernay. In 1557 the participated in the siege of Thionville, near Calais. He died there the following year.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is generally credited as the inventor of the dragoon military speciality (arquebusiers à cheval or horse arquibusiers).
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Filippo was also a military commander, as was his brother Leone Strozzi, a Knight of Malta, known as the Prior of Capua.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Voir [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre_Strozzi '''Wikipedia...''']
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Stròzzi, Piero. - Condottiero (Firenze 1510 circa - Thionville 1558), figlio di Giovan Battista detto Filippo, e fratello di Leone. Abbandonato lo stato ecclesiastico e abbracciata la carriera militare, si pose al servizio dei Fra
2 CONC ncesi per abbattere in Italia il predominio di Carlo V, protettore dei Medici. Alla morte del duca Alessandro, propose ai fuorusciti fiorentini di attaccare il nuovo duca Cosimo I. Il tentativo fallì con la rotta di Montemurlo (15
2 CONC 37), e Piero dovette fuggire a Venezia e quindi in Francia. Entrò al servizio di Francesco I, e fece le campagne in Italia (1544, 1551, 1554), recandosi al soccorso di Siena, assediata dal duca Cosimo, alleato di Carlo V. Dopo la 
2 CONC capitolazione di Siena, continuò la resistenza a Montalcino (1556); ebbe da Enrico II, a riconoscimento del suo valore, il titolo di maresciallo di Francia. Luogotenente generale delle truppe pontificie (1557), sbloccò Ostia, mina
2 CONC cciata dagli Imperiali, salvando così Roma. Dopo la sconfitta di San Quintino fu richiamato in Francia, dove (1557) prese parte all'assedio di Calais; l'anno seguente fu mortalmente ferito davanti a Thionville, in Lorena.
