1 NOTE {geni:about_me} - http://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/savoia-carignano-emanuele-filiberto-amedeo-di_%28Dizionario-Biografico%29/
2 CONT
2 CONT - http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emmanuel_Philibert_of_Savoy,_Prince_of_Carignan
2 CONT
2 CONT Emmanuel Philibert, Prince of Carignan
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Emmanuel Philibert of Savoy, Prince of Carignan)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Emmanuel Philbert
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Carignan
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Maria Angela Caterina d'Este
2 CONT
2 CONT Detail
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Victor Amadeus, Prince of Carignan
2 CONT
2 CONT Full name
2 CONT
2 CONT Italian: Emanuele Filiberto di Savoia
2 CONT
2 CONT French: Emmanuel Philibert de Savoie
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Thomas Francis, Prince of Carignan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Marie de Bourbon
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	20 August 1628(1628-08-20)
2 CONT
2 CONT Moutiers, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	21 April 1709 (aged 80)
2 CONT
2 CONT Palazzo Carignano, Turin, Italy
2 CONT
2 CONT Emmanuel Philibert of Savoy (20 August 1628 – 21 April 1709), Prince of Carignan, was the son and heir of Thomas Francis, Prince of Carignan. It was he who constructed the Palazzo Carignano in Turin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Titles, styles, honours and arms
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 3.1 Titles and styles
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 References and notes
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in France a deaf and mute, a condition which gave his family great concern. However he managed to learn to communicate with others by lip-reading, and to speak a few words, though with great difficulty.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a youth he was sent to the Spanish priest Don Manuel Ramirez, a famous teacher of the deaf-mute in Spain. Under his guidance Emmanuel Philibert learned to read and to write. He went on to study a range of sciences under the gui
2 CONC dance of Alessandro Tesauro, showing great aptitude. His sister, Princess Louise Christine was the wife of Hereditary Prince Ferdinand Maximilian of Baden-Baden, they were the parents of the famous Türkenlouis, Ludwig Wilhelm of B
2 CONC aden-Baden.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his 20s Emmanuel Philibert followed his father Thomas in the last of his campaigns in Lombardy, acquitting himself with great valour, and two years later he was named a colonel of cavalry in the service of his distant cousin Lo
2 CONC uis XIV, King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1658 Emmanuel Philibert was created a lieutenant-general by his first cousin Charles Emmanuel II, Duke of Savoy, in the latter's absence, and in 1663 was appointed governor of the city of Asti. When Charles Emmanuel died in 167
2 CONC 5, his son and heir Victor Amadeus II was only nine years old, and Emmanuel Philibert became heir presumptive to the duchy of Savoy unless and until Victor Amadeus had a male heir in turn (which was not to happen until 1699).
2 CONT
2 CONT A great connoisseur of architecture, Emmanuel Philibert commissioned the Palazzo Carignano in Turin, built between 1679 and 1684. He also commissioned major renovations to the castle of Racconigi. Guarino Guarini rebuilt an older 
2 CONC dwelling, while the project for the park was entrusted to André le Nôtre who realised magnificent French-style gardens.
2 CONT
2 CONT In November 1701, he acted as Philip V of Spain in a proxy marriage between Philip V and his cousin Maria Luisa of Savoy.[1] He also acted as god father to Maria Luisa's sister, Princess Maria Adeliade, mother of Louis XV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emmanuel Philibert died in Turin on 21 April 1709. In 1836 his remains were brought to the church of San Michele della Chiusa in that city.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria Angela Caterina by a follower of Rigaud.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 10 November 1684 in the Castle of Racconigi, Emmanuel Philibert, by now in his fifties, married Maria Angela Caterina d'Este, the beautiful daughter of the late General Borso d'Este, marquis of Scandiano, the collateral branch 
2 CONC of the duchy of Modena, and Ippolita d'Este. This match was opposed by Louis XIV of France, who had wanted Emmanuel Philibert to marry a French princess, given his position as heir to the duchy of Savoy (Marie Thérèse de Bourbon o
2 CONC r one of her sisters were the proposed bride's, as Louis XIV had no legitimate daughters).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1685, after the intercession of Vittorio Amadeo II, Emmanuel Philibert obtained permission from Louis XIV to return to Turin. He and Caterina had two girls and two boys, of whom only their son Vittorio Amadeo would have childre
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Maria Isabella of Savoy (14 March 1687 – 2 May 1767) married:
2 CONT
2 CONT         Alfonso Tapparello, Count of Lagnasco, no issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT         Conte Eugenio Ruffia di Cambiano, no issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT         Cavaliere Carlo Biandrate, no issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Maria Vittoria of Savoy (12 Febusry 1688 – 18 May 1763) married Onorato Malabayla, Count of Canale in 1721, had no issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Victor Amadeus of Savoy, (1 Mar 1690 – 4 April 1741) Prince of Carignan married Maria Vittoria Francesca of Savoy, illegitimate daughter of Victor Amadeus II of Sardinia and his mistress Jeanne Baptiste d'Albert de Luynes, h
2 CONC ad issue;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Thomas Philippe Geatano of Savoy (10 May 1696 – 12 Sep 1715) never married;
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Titles, styles, honours and arms
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Titles and styles
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 20 August 1628 – 22 January 1656 His Highness Prince Emmanuel Philibert of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 22 Janusry 1656 – 21 April 1709 His Highness the Prince of Carignan
