2 CONT 𝐌𝐚𝐫𝐢𝐞 𝐋𝐨𝐮𝐢𝐬𝐞 𝐝’𝐎𝐫𝐥𝐞𝐚𝐧𝐬, 𝐐𝐮𝐞𝐞𝐧 𝐨𝐟 𝐒𝐩𝐚𝐢𝐧
2 CONT (𝟐𝟔 𝐌𝐚𝐫𝐜𝐡 𝟏𝟔𝟔𝟐 – 𝟏𝟐 𝐅𝐞𝐛𝐫𝐮𝐚𝐫𝐲 𝟏𝟔𝟖𝟗)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Louise d’Orléans was born #onthisday as the eldest daughter of Philippe of France, Duke of Orléans and his first wife, Princess Henrietta of England. Charming, pretty and graceful, Marie Louise, who was her father’s favourit
2 CONC e child, had a happy childhood. Then, her mother died in 1670. The following year, her father married. All her life, Marie Louise maintained an affectionate correspondence with her stepmother.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1679, Marie was informed of her betrothal to Charles II of Spain. Distressed by the arranged marriage, she spent most of her time weeping, since she had fallen in love with her cousin Louis. But her new husband had fallen in lo
2 CONC ve with her and remained so until the end of his life. However, the confining etiquette of the Spanish Court (e.g., touching the Queen was forbidden) and unsuccessful attempts to bear a child caused her distress. After ten years o
2 CONC f marriage the couple had no children. Marie Louise confided to the French ambassador, that ”she was really not a virgin any longer, but that as far as she could figure things, she believed she would never have children.”
2 CONT
2 CONT During the last years of her life she became overweight. She was reportedly fond of sweetened lemon and cinnamon drinks, the making of which required 32 pounds of sugar. After horseback riding in 1689, she experienced abdominal pa
2 CONC in followed by convulsions and vomiting. After doctors found that her condition had become mortal, confessors were called in to administer last rites. On her deathbed, she spoke to her husband the following words, ”Many women may 
2 CONC be with His Majesty, but none will love him more than I do.” She died the following night. There were rumors she was poisoned. She rests in the Monastery of El Escorial.
