1 NOTE Although her father was a convert to Roman Catholicism, Mary was brought 
2 CONT up as a Protestant and was married at the age of 15 to the Dutch 
2 CONT Protestant prince William of Orange. In 1688, English opponents of James, 
2 CONT unhappy with his autocratic rule and favoritism toward Roman Catholics, 
2 CONT initiated the Glorious Revolution, forcing James into exile and giving the 
2 CONT throne to Mary and William (who became king as William III). They were 
2 CONT crowned as joint rulers in April 1689. Mary governed as regent while 
2 CONT William was campaigning in Ireland (1690-91) and on the Continent 
2 CONT (1692-94), but for the most part she simply carried out policies 
2 CONT formulated by her husband. William continued to rule alone after her 
2 CONT death.
1 NOTE Ruled after her father was dethroned. Ceded her power to her husband and cousin William II of England
1 NOTE ACCEDED 2/13/1689 (CROWNED WESTMINSTER); RULED JOINTLY WITH WILLIAM III FROM
2 CONT 1689-1694
