1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Francis_Edward_Stuart
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince James, Prince of Wales (James Francis Edward Stuart; "The Old Pretender" or "The Old Chevalier"; 10 June 1688 – 1 January 1766) was the son of the deposed James II and VII. As such, he claimed the English, Scottish and Iris
2 CONC h thrones (as James III and VIII) from the death of his father in 1701, when he was proclaimed king of England, Scotland and Ireland by his cousin Louis XIV of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Francis Edward, about 1703, portrait in the Royal Collection attributed to Alexis Simon BelleFrom the moment of his birth, on 10 June 1688, at St. James's Palace, the prince was the subject of controversy. He was born to the
2 CONC  reigning king, James II of England and VII of Scots, and his Roman Catholic second wife, Mary of Modena, and as such was automatically Duke of Cornwall and Duke of Rothesay among other titles.
2 CONT
2 CONT James II had two adult daughters from his first marriage who had been brought up in the Protestant faith. As long as there was a possibility of one of them succeeding him directly, his opponents saw his rule as only a temporary se
2 CONC tback. When people began to fear that Mary would produce a son and heir, a movement grew to replace James by force with his elder daughter Princess Mary and his son-in-law/nephew, William of Orange.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the young prince was born, a false rumour was immediately spread that the call for a "warming-pan" had been the pretext for a substitution, the real baby having allegedly been born dead. On 10 December, within six months of h
2 CONC is birth, Mary of Modena left London and took him to France for safety, while his father continued to fight (unsuccessfully) to retain his crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT With his sister Louisa Maria, the prince was brought up in France. There, recognised by King Louis XIV of France as the rightful heir to the English and Scottish thrones, he became the focus for the Jacobite movement.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Stuart, the "Old Pretender."On his father's death in 1701, he declared himself King, with the name of James III and VIII and recognised as such by France, Spain, the Papal States and Modena. All of these states refused to re
2 CONC cognise William III, Mary II or Queen Anne as the legitimate British sovereign. As a result of this, he was attainted for treason, 2 March 1702, and his titles forfeited under British law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Having been delayed in France by an attack of measles, James attempted an invasion, trying to land at the Firth of Forth on 23 March 1708. His French ships were driven back by the fleet of Admiral Sir George Byng.
2 CONT
2 CONT Had he renounced his Roman Catholic faith, he might have strengthened the existing support of Tory, pro-Restoration forces in England,[2] but he refused to do so. As a result, in 1714, a German Protestant became King—George I of G
2 CONC reat Britain.
2 CONT
2 CONT French forces were defeated, and Louis XIV of France was forced to accept peace with England and her allies. He signed the Treaty of Utrecht, in 1713, that, amongst other conditions, required him to expel James from France.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the following year, the Jacobites started "The 'Fifteen" Jacobite rising in Scotland, aimed at restoring "James III and VIII" to the throne. In 1715, James finally set foot on Scottish soil, following the indecisive Battle of S
2 CONC heriffmuir, but he was disappointed by the strength of support he found. Instead of carrying through the plans for a coronation at Scone, he returned to France, sailing from Montrose. He was not welcomed back, because his patron, 
2 CONC Louis XIV, was dead and the government found him a political embarrassment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pope Clement XI offered James the Palazzo Muti in Rome as his residence, and he accepted. Innocent XIII, like his predecessor, showed much support. Thanks to the mediation of a close friend of his, Cardinal Filippo Antonio Gualter
2 CONC io, James was granted a life annuity of eight thousand Roman scudi. Such help enabled him to organise a Roman Jacobite court, where the Pope's cousin, Francesco Maria Conti of Siena, was the Gentiluomo di camera (Chamberlain).
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 September 1719, James Francis Edward Stuart married Maria Clementina Sobieska (1702–35), granddaughter of the Polish king, John III Sobieski. They had two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Edward Stuart, (31 December 1720 – 31 January 1788), aka "Bonnie Prince Charlie" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Benedict Stuart, (11 March 1725 – 13 July 1807), Cardinal of the Roman Catholic Church 
2 CONT
2 CONT Following James's failure, attention turned to his son Charles, "the Young Pretender", whose rebellion of 1745 came closer to success than his father's. With the failure of this second rebellion, however, the Stuart hopes of regai
2 CONC ning the British throne were effectively destroyed.
2 CONT
2 CONT James died in Rome on 1 January 1766, and was buried in St. Peter's Basilica at the Vatican. From 14 January the Papacy recognized the Hanoverian dynasty as the legitimate rulers of Britain and Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his father's deposition he lost his automatic titles as eldest son of the Sovereign (i.e. Duke of Cornwall, Duke of Rothesay, Earl of Carrick, Baron Renfrew, Lord of the Isles, Prince and Great Steward of Scotland). Thus he w
2 CONC as Prince James Francis Edward, Prince of Wales and Earl of Chester, Knight Companion of the Most Noble Order of the Garter until his attainture for treason.
2 CONT
2 CONT KG: Knight of the Garter, 1692 – 2 March 1702 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Born on a Sunday at 10:00 am. Raised at Saint-Germain-en-Laye near Paris. Christened Oct. 1688 in England & blessed by Papal Nuncio. Frequently called 'James Edward Stuart'. Aka 'The Pretender' to the throne, Jacabite 'James III' 
2 CONC & the 'Chevalier de Saint George'. Tall & lean in figure, black hair/black eyes & dark complexion. He had been a sickly child (lack of oxygen as an infant & a rash). Burial ~ a marble tomb shared with his sons Charles Edward & Hen
2 CONC ry. It was paid for by King George III.
2 CONT --------------------
