1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Jean_Marie_de_Bourbon,_Duke_of_Penthi%C3%A8vre English - Wikipedia...], [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Jean_Marie_de_Bourbon - Français...]
2 CONT * [http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Chateau-sceaux-colbert.jpg '''Castle Sceaux'''], [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Ch%C3%A2teau_de_Rambouillet.jpg '''Castle Rambouillet'''] - castles where he lived.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Louis Jean Marie de Bourbon (16 November 1725 – 4 March 1793) was the son of Louis Alexandre de Bourbon and his wife Marie Victoire de Noailles. He was also a grandson of Louis XIV of France and his mistress, Madame de Montespan
2 CONC . From birth he was known as the Duke of Penthièvre. He also possessed the following titles: Prince of Lamballe (given later as a courtesy title to the duke's only surviving son); Prince of Carignano; Duke of Rambouillet; Duke of 
2 CONC Aumale (1775); Duke of Gisors; Duke of Châteauvillain; Duke of Arc-en-Barrois; Duke of Amboise; Count of Eu; Count of Guingamp. He was the father in law of Philippe Égalité. '''Biography'''
2 CONT * Louis Jean Marie de Bourbon was born at the [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Ch%C3%A2teau_de_Rambouillet.jpg '''Château de Rambouillet'''], the son of Louis XIV's youngest legitimised son with Madame de Montespan, the Count of
2 CONC  Toulouse, and his wife, Marie Victoire de Noailles, one of the daughters of Anne Jules de Noailles, Duke of Noailles. Since his mother acted as a surrogate parent to the young, orphaned Louis XV, the duke formed a close relations
2 CONC hip with the young monarch, who was his godfather.
2 CONT * At the age of twelve, upon his father's death, he succeeded to his father's military posts and titles:
2 CONT * On 2 July 1733 at the age of six, he was made a maréchal de camp (field marshal) and the next year, a lieutenant général (lieutenant general). '''In 1740, he received the Ordre de la Toison d'or''' from the king. In 1742, the ki
2 CONC ng conferred upon him the Order of the Holy Spirit. He served in the military under his maternal uncle, the maréchal-duc de Noailles, and fought brilliantly at Dettingen in 1743 and Fontenoy in 1745.
2 CONT * As the possessor of one of the largest fortunes in Europe, Louis Jean Marie was a very attractive marriage candidate, especially considering his close links with the French royal family.
2 CONT * A suggestion was made that he marry his cousin, Louise Henriette de Bourbon, the eldest granddaughter of his paternal aunt, Louise-Françoise de Bourbon. This idea, however, was abandoned as Louise Henriette's mother wished her d
2 CONC aughter to marry Louis Philippe d'Orléans, the heir of the House of Orléans.
2 CONT * In 1744, at the age of nineteen, Penthièvre married Princess Maria Teresa Felicitas of Modena, (1726-1754), the daughter of Francesco III d'Este, the sovereign Duke of Modena and Reggio, and his first cousin, Charlotte Aglaé d'O
2 CONC rléans. The young couple occupied a suite of apartments at Versailles which had previously been occupied by their joint ancestor Madame de Montespan. These apartments were used by the duke and his family until the reign of Louis X
2 CONC VI of France when the space was given to Mesdames, the new king's spinster aunts.
2 CONT * The couple had seven children, only two of whom survived infancy. The Duchess of Penthièvre died in childbirth in 1754.
2 CONT * Very badly hurt by the loss of his young wife, the duke never married again.
2 CONT * After his wife's death, the duke lived increasingly away from the court at Versailles, dividing his time between two of his many country residences, the '''Château de Rambouillet''' and the [http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia
2 CONC /commons/7/77/Chateau-sceaux-colbert.jpg '''Château de Sceaux''']. He devoted the majority of the rest of his life to dispensing charity. During the French Revolution, he gave refuge in Sceaux to the poet Jean Pierre Claris de Flo
2 CONC rian, who had formerly been one of his pages and secretaries at the Château d'Anet.
2 CONT * In 1791, he moved to the Château de Bizy,[1] at Vernon in Normandy,[2] where his daughter joined him in April of that year after leaving her husband, the duc d'Orléans. Greatly respected by the people because of his philanthropy
2 CONC , the duke was never bothered by the radicals as the French Revolution progressed. Others in his immediate family, however, were not spared. On 3 September 1792, his daughter-in-law, the princesse de Lamballe, was savagely murdere
2 CONC d, and on 21 January 1793, his cousin Louis XVI was executed. He never saw the arrest of his daughter in April 1793, as he died on 4 March 1793 at Bizy. On the night of 6 to 7 March, his body was brought clandestinely to Dreux, wh
2 CONC ere it was buried in the family crypt at the Collégiale Saint-Étienne. During his lifetime, the duc de Penthièvre had had one passion, that of collecting watches.
2 CONT ====Legacy====
2 CONT * Louis Jean Marie de Bourbon was also the head and founder of the house of Bourbon-Penthièvre, an extinct and illegitimate branch of the House of Bourbon, which was originally called the House of Bourbon-Toulouse after the title 
2 CONC of his father Louis-Alexandre de Bourbon, comte de Toulouse.
2 CONT * Penthièvre was one of the wealthiest men of his day and probably the richest in France. He was known to be very charitable. Most of his vast riches derived from the fortune of La Grande Mademoiselle, the first cousin of King Lou
2 CONC is XIV.
2 CONT * In 1681, Louis XIV had given his consent that his cousin marry the duc de Lauzun, the only man she ever loved, on the condition she make the Duke of Maine, the newly legitimised son of the king and Mme de Montespan, her heir. Al
2 CONC l she could be made to accept, against her will, was to give the young duke the county of Eu and the principality of Dombes. Upon which, untrue to his word, Louis XIV refused to let her marry Lauzun.[3][4]
2 CONT * Du Maine's fortune was enlarged with many expensive gifts from his adoring father. His two sons inherited his fortune and when they both died childless, the duc de Penthièvre was the sole heir to du Maine's wealth. The châteaux 
2 CONC at Sceaux, Anet, Aumale, Dreux and Gisors were part of this huge inheritance.
2 CONT * In addition, being his only child, the duc de Penthièvre was the only heir of the comte de Toulouse from whom he inherited the Hôtel de Toulouse in Paris, and the château de Rambouillet surrounded by the game-rich Rambouillet fo
2 CONC rest. The Hôtel de Toulouse was the family's residence in Paris. The duc de Penthièvre willed it to his daughter, Louise Marie Adélaïde de Bourbon-Penthièvre. It was located opposite the Palais-Royal.[5]
2 CONT ====Over the years, the duc de Penthièvre also acquired other estates:====
2 CONT *château de Chanteloup, a vast château[6] situated in the Touraine province of France. He received this after the death of the duc de Choiseul, in 1785,
2 CONT * château d'Amboise, a castle in the Loire Valley,
2 CONT * château at Châteauneuf-sur-Loire,
2 CONT * château de La Ferté-Vidame, this château was home to the sharp-penned memorialist, Saint-Simon,
2 CONT * Because his male heir, the prince de Lamballe, predeceased him in 1768, his only surviving child, his daughter, became the sole heir to his fortune. In 1769, she had married the Duke of Chartres, future duc d'Orléans, known to h
2 CONC istory as Philippe Égalité. As a result, what she managed (after the Bourbon Restoration) to recuperate of her fortune confiscated during the French Revolution, passed, upon her death in 1821, into the possession of the House of O
2 CONC rléans.
2 CONT * The lands of the Comté de Dreux (County of Dreux), had been given to the duc de Penthièvre by his cousin Louis XVI. In November 1783, after having sold to Louis XVI the château de Rambouillet and the immense rich-game forest att
2 CONC ached to the estate - the latter being the main reason of the sale - Penthièvre transferred the nine bodies of his family (his parents, his wife and six of his seven children) from the 12th century Saint-Lubin church in the villag
2 CONC e of Rambouillet to the Collégiale Saint-Étienne de Dreux, where he himself was buried in March 1793. On November 1793, a revolutionary mob desecrated the family crypt and threw the ten bodies in a mass grave in the Collégiale cem
2 CONC etery (cimetière des Chanoines). In 1816, his daughter, Louise Marie Adélaïde de Bourbon, duchesse douairière d'Orléans, had a new chapel built on the site of that grave, as the final resting place for the members of the House of 
2 CONC Bourbon-Toulouse-Penthièvre. After the accession to the throne of her son, Louis-Philippe, King of the French, the chapel was named Chapelle Royale de Dreux,[7] and became the necropolis of the royal Orléans family. It contains th
2 CONC e remains of 75 members of the Bourbon-Penthièvre and Orléans families.
2 CONT * A street in Paris near the Avenue des Champs Élysées is named for the duc de Penthièvre.[8] At the site of n° 11 rue de Penthièvre, an hôtel particulier of the First French Empire period with a large garden, is believed to have 
2 CONC once been the residence of the duc de Penthièvre's grandson, the future King of the French, Louis-Philippe, in his youth. In the late 19th century, n° 11 housed the American Embassy. This address became famous in the early 20th ce
2 CONC ntury as the salon de couture of the British designer, "Lucile".
2 CONT ====Issue====
2 CONT
2 CONT     Louis Marie de Bourbon, duc de Rambouillet (1746–1749);
2 CONT     Louis Alexandre Joseph Stanislas de Bourbon, prince de Lamballe (1747–1768), married Princess Marie Louise of Savoy (1749–1792) and had no issue;
2 CONT     Jean Marie de Bourbon (1748–1755), duc de Châteauvillain ;
2 CONT     Vincent Marie Louis de Bourbon (1750–1752), comte de Guingamp ;
2 CONT     Marie Louise de Bourbon (1751–1753) ;
2 CONT     Louise Marie Adélaïde de Bourbon, Mademoiselle de Penthièvre (1753–1821), married Philippe d'Orléans and had issue;
2 CONT     Louis Marie Félicité de Bourbon (1754), died at birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT     16 November 1725 – 4 March 1793 His Serene Highness the Duke of Penthièvre
2 CONT
2 CONT Golden Fleece - Knights: Spanish Branch
2 CONT
