1 NAME Cardinal Henry Benedict Stuart, Duke of York
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 6 MAR 1725
2 ADDR
3 CITY Roma
3 CTRY Italia
1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 JUL 1807
2 ADDR
3 CITY Frascati,
3 CTRY Italia
1 OCCU Cardinal-Bishop of Frascati (1761–1803) Cardinal-Priest of Santa Maria in Trastevere (1759–1761) Titular Archbishop of Corinthus (1758–1761) Cardinal-Priest of Santi Apostoli (1752–1761) Cardinal-Priest of Santa Maria in Portico (
2 CONC Campitelli) (1748
1 TITL His Eminence,
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Benedict_Stuart
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Benedict Stuart (11 March 1725 – 13 July 1807) was a Roman Catholic Cardinal, as well as the fourth and final Jacobite heir to publicly claim the thrones of England, Scotland, and Ireland. Unlike his father, James Francis Ed
2 CONC ward Stuart, and brother, Charles Edward Stuart, Henry made no effort to seize the throne. After Charles's death in January 1788 the Papacy did not recognise Henry as the lawful ruler of England, Scotland and Ireland, but referred
2 CONC  to him as the Cardinal Duke of York.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He spent his life in the Papal States and had a long career in the clergy of the Roman Catholic Church, rising to become the Dean of the College of Cardinals and Cardinal-Bishop of Ostia and Velletri. At the time of his death he w
2 CONC as (and still is) one of the longest serving Cardinals in the Church's history.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his youth, Henry's father made him Duke of York (in the Jacobite peerage), and it was by this title that he was best known. Upon the death of his brother in 1788 Henry became known by Jacobites, and within his personal entourag
2 CONC e, as Henry IX of England, although publicly he referred to himself as Cardinal-Duke of York nuncupatus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Benedict Maria Clement Thomas Francis Xavier Stuart was born in exile at Rome on 6 March 1725 and baptized on the same day by Pope Benedict XIII, 37 years after his grandfather James II and VII lost the throne, and ten years
2 CONC  after his father's failed attempt to regain it. His father was James Francis Edward Stuart, known to his opponents as "the Old Pretender". His mother was the Princess Maria Klementyna Sobieska, granddaughter of the Polish King, J
2 CONC ohn III Sobieski.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry went to France in 1745 to help his brother, Prince Charles Edward Stuart ("Bonnie Prince Charlie", or "the Young Pretender") prepare the Jacobite campaign of that year. After its defeat, Henry Stuart returned to Italy. On 30
2 CONC  June 1747 Pope Benedict XIV conferred him with tonsure and created him Cardinal-Deacon of S. Maria in Portico in special consistory held on the 3 July 1747. On 27 August 1747 he was promoted to the four minor orders by the Pope. 
2 CONC He received the subdiaconate on 18 August 1748 and diaconate on 25 August 1748. He was ordained priest on 1 September 1748 and consecrated titular Archbishop of Corinth on 2 October 1758.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was advanced to the order of Cardinal Priest in 1748, maintaining title to S. Maria in Portico. In 1752 he transferred to the titulus of Ss. XII Apostoli. He was made Cardinal-Bishop of Frascati on 13 July 1761, and eventually 
2 CONC succeeded to the See of Ostia and Velletri on his appointment as Dean of the Sacred College of Cardinals on 26 September 1803. He lived and worked in Frascati for many years, descending each afternoon in his carriage to Rome, wher
2 CONC e his position as vice-chancellor entitled him to the Palazzo della Cancelleria.
2 CONT
2 CONT His revenues from the multitudinous ecclesiastical preferments he enjoyed were enormous. His income from abbeys and other pluralities in Flanders, Spain, Naples and France amounted to 40,000 Pounds in English money at the time. He
2 CONC  also held sinecure benefices yielding revenues in Spanish America. He owned territory in Mexico, which contributed largely to his income.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was the last claimant to the English throne to touch for the King's Evil.
2 CONT
2 CONT French Revolution and later life
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of the French Revolution, he lost his French Royal benefices and sacrificed many other resources to assist Pope Pius VI. This, in addition to the seizure of his Frascati property by the French, caused him to descend in
2 CONC to poverty. The British Minister in Venice arranged for Henry to receive an annuity of £4,000 from King George III of Great Britain. Although the British government represented this as an act of charity, Henry and the Jacobites co
2 CONC nsidered it to be a first installment on the money which was legally owed to him. (For many years the British government had promised to return the English dowry of his grandmother, Mary of Modena, but had never actually done so.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry returned to Frascati in 1803. In September of that year he became the Dean of the College of Cardinals and hence Cardinal Bishop of Ostia and Velletri, though he still lived in the episcopal palace at Frascati. He died there
2 CONC  on 13 July 1807, aged 82.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal relationships
2 CONT
2 CONT Historians have drawn upon contemporary perceptions to explore the suggestion that Henry was homosexual[3]. These accounts include the writings of Hester Lynch Thrale[4] (1741-1821), and the diplomat and writer Giuseppe Gorani[5] 
2 CONC (1740-1819). However, Gorani admitted to not having gathered sufficient evidence to confirm his suspicions either way.
2 CONT
2 CONT The writer Gaetano Moroni[6] provides the lengthiest account of Henry’s close attachment with Monsignor Lercari, his majordomo. This led to tensions between the cardinal and his father who tried to have Lercari dismissed from serv
2 CONC ice and sent from Rome. A public scandal was only narrowly avoided after the personal intervention of Pope Benedict XIV.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1769 onwards Henry remained close to Monsignor Angelo Cesarini, a nobleman from Perugia, who thanks to Henry’s protection, won various honours, was made canon of the cathedral in Frascati, and finally in 1801 became Bishop of
2 CONC  Milevi. When Henry died, Cesarini was still at his side, as he had been for 40 years. Caution should be given, however, against assuming any active sexual relationships, because equally clear in contemporary sources is York's pro
2 CONC per and virtuous nature, and horror of all impropriety.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Post mortem
2 CONT
2 CONT Under his will, which he signed as "Henry R", he was succeeded in all his claimed British rights by his friend and nearest blood-relative, Charles Emmanuel IV of Sardinia. But Charles never asserted nor renounced his Jacobite clai
2 CONC ms, nor have any of his successors to this day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contrary to popular belief, he did not leave the Crown Jewels to the Prince of Wales, afterwards George IV of the United Kingdom. All his property was entrusted to Monsignor Angelo Cesarini, for distribution. Cesarini sent the Pri
2 CONC nce of Wales several jewels from Henry's private collection. These included a "Lesser George" (thought to have been worn by Charles I at his execution, and now at Windsor Castle) and a St Andrew's Cross (now at Edinburgh Castle ])
2 CONC , which are insignia of the orders of the Garter and the Thistle, and also a ruby ring.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Benedict, his brother, his father and his mother are buried in the crypt of St. Peter's Basilica in the Vatican. There is a monument to the Royal Stuarts designed by Antonio Canova in the basilica to their memory on one of t
2 CONC he columns in the basilica proper. This was restored within living memory at the expense of Queen Elizabeth the Queen Mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his life, Cardinal Stuart was assigned the following Diaconia and Tituli:
2 CONT
2 CONT 13 July 1747 Cardinal Deacon of Santa Maria in Portico
2 CONT
2 CONT 16 September 1748 Cardinal Priest of Santa Maria in Portico
2 CONT
2 CONT 18 December 1752 Cardinal Priest of Santi XII Apostoli
2 CONT
2 CONT 12 February 1759 Cardinal Priest of Santa Maria in Trastevere
2 CONT
2 CONT 13 July 1761 Cardinal Bishop of Frascati
2 CONT
2 CONT 14 January 1763 Comendatario of San Lorenzo in Damaso (proper of the cardinal vice-chancellor, held in addition to the suburbicarian sees)
2 CONT
2 CONT 26 September 1803 Cardinal Bishop of Ostia e Velletri (proper of the dean of the Sacred College of Cardinals)
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1774 he became Sub-dean, and on 15 September, 1803 - Dean of the Sacred College of Cardinals.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a cardinal elector in the papal conclaves of 1758, 1769, 1774-75 and 1799-1800.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms
2 CONT
2 CONT During the pretence of his father and brother, Henry claimed a coat of arms consisting of those of the kingdom, differenced by a crescent argent[8] or white crescent.
