1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Louise Élisabeth de Croÿ, Marquise (later Duchess) of Tourzel (Louise Élisabeth Félicité Françoise Armande Anne Marie Jeanne Joséphine; 11 June 1749 – 15 May 1832) was a French memoir-writer, noble and courtier. Sh
2 CONC e was the last governess to the royal children of King Louis XVI of France and his wife, Queen Marie Antoinette.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life[edit source]
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise Élisabeth was born in Paris, into the illustrious Croÿ family during the reign of Louis XV. The Duchess's father was the Duke Louis Ferdinand Joseph of Havré and his mother the Princess Marie Louise of Montmorency-Luxembour
2 CONC g. She was married in 1766, at the age of seventeen, to the Marquis de Tourzel. They enjoyed a happy marriage for twenty years, in which Louise Élisabeth bore six children. Her husband was killed in a hunting accident in 1786.[1] 
2 CONC She was a staunch supporter of the House of Bourbon, and had this motto engraved on a ring she refused to part with: Lord, save the King, the Dauphin, and his sister![2]
2 CONT
2 CONT French Revolution[edit source]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1789, after the fall of the Bastille, many members of the Queen's intimate circle were forced to flee abroad. The Duchesse de Polignac, the queen's favourite and the governess to the royal children, was forced to emigrate to Sw
2 CONC itzerland.[3] Marie Antoinette appointed Louise Élisabeth to the newly vacant post, with particular attention to be paid to the Dauphin, Louis-Charles. The Marquise was advised to curb the Dauphin's fear of loud noises, particular
2 CONC ly the barking of the many dogs at Versailles.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT From this intimate position, the Marquise de Tourzel was able to watch the disintegration of the Ancien Régime. After an angry mob of hungry women incited by revolutionaries stormed the Palace of Versailles on October 5, 1789, the
2 CONC  Marquise accompanied the royal family to live in the Tuileries Palace in Paris.[4] Tourzel's loyalty was strong, and she refused to abandon the royal children as political strife in the nation dramatically increased. She even acc
2 CONC ompanied the King and his family on a dangerous attempt to flee Paris for a royalist stronghold in Montmédy.[5] This attempt failed, and the entire party was dragged back to Paris by republicans.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the abolition of the monarchy in 1792, Tourzel was separated from the royal family and imprisoned in La Force Prison and the Prison Port-Libre.[6] Also imprisoned at the same time were Tourzel's daughter, Pauline de Tourzel,
2 CONC  and Marie Antoinette's most loyal friend, the Princesse de Lamballe.[6] Shortly after their imprisonment, the three women found themselves victims of the September Massacres, when thousands of incarcerated people in Paris were ma
2 CONC ssacred by violent revolutionaries who were trying to rid the prisons of jailed aristocrats. Tourzel and her daughter were smuggled out of the prison by a mysterious gentleman, but Lamballe was not so fortunate.[4] She was savagel
2 CONC y murdered, and her decapitated head was then paraded around the city.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1793, Louis XVI was executed.[8] In October, Queen Marie Antoinette was also sent to the guillotine.[8] Tourzel was devastated by their deaths, and she was equally shocked to hear of the death of Louis-Charles in 1795.[
2 CONC 4] Several times over the coming decades, Tourzel was accosted by various men pretending to be "Louis XVII of France".[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Post revolution[edit source]
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Bourbon Restoration, Tourzel was made a duchess by a grateful King Charles X.[9] She later published her memoirs, which are an invaluable historical account of the final days of the royal household.[1] Her daughter, Pau
2 CONC line, became a lady-in-waiting to Marie Antoinette's only surviving child, Marie-Thérèse-Charlotte, Duchesse d'Angoulême.
2 CONT
2 CONT In fiction[edit source]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Marquise has featured in several novels about the French Royal family, including Trianon and Madame Royale by Elena Maria Vidal, Flaunting, Extravagant Queen by Jean Plaidy and the Marie Antoinette romances by Alexandre Dumas,
2 CONC  père. The character of the Marquise de Tourzel appeared in the 1956 French film Marie-Antoinette reine de France.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT References[edit source]
2 CONT
