1 NOTE He was also as Ferdinand IV he was also king of Naples (1759-1806, 
2 CONT 1815-25), and as Ferdinand III, king of Sicily (1806-15). In 1759 
2 CONT Ferdinand became king of Naples. He ruled for eight years under the 
2 CONT regency of his father's chief minister Bernardo Tanucci (1698-1783). In 
2 CONT 1768 he married the daughter of Maria Theresa, empress of Austria, and 
2 CONT replaced Tanucci with John Francis Edward Acton (1736-1811), an 
2 CONT Englishman. Influenced by his wife and by Acton, Ferdinand allied Naples 
2 CONT with the coalition opposing France in the French Revolution and the 
2 CONT Napoleonic Wars. The French captured Naples in 1799 and there set up the 
2 CONT Parthenopean Republic. Ferdinand found refuge in Palermo, Sicily, until an 
2 CONT army under Cardinal Fabrizio Ruffo (1744-1827) recovered Naples later that 
2 CONT year. Ferdinand's return was marked by mass executions of Neapolitans who 
2 CONT had sided with the French. In 1806 he fled once more to Sicily before the 
2 CONT advance of Joseph Bonaparte, who had been made king of Naples by his 
2 CONT brother, Napoleon, and who captured Naples soon thereafter. Ferdinand's 
2 CONT authority was limited to Sicily from 1806 until 1815; his reign was 
2 CONT unpopular, and for a time (1812) his son acted for him as regent. 
2 CONT Ferdinand returned to Naples after Napoleon's overthrow in 1815. The 
2 CONT following year, against the will of most of his subjects, he reconstituted 
2 CONT the kingdom of the Two Sicilies along autocratic lines with the aid of 
2 CONT Austria. He was succeeded by his son, Francis I.
