1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The following story is titled "The Weltons 1756" It was a practice of the settlers on the south branch of the Potomac river to leave the fort in numbers and assist others with harvest. About the year 1756 a party o
2 CONC f 9 whites left Fort George to assist Mr Job Welton to cut his fathers meadow and hunt cattle. After collecting cattle and cutting the meadow they took shelter as night fell under a large elm tree. They were aroused sometime later
2 CONC  by the crack of a rifle. Mr Welton was laying with his brother Jonathon who was shot through the heart. The party sprang to their feet and attempted to flee. In his alarm Mr Job Welton forgot his rifle and fled in the company of 
2 CONC Mr Delay. Mr Welton and Mr Delay ran about 200 yards pursued by an Indian when Mr Delay wheeled around and fired his rifle, which brought down their pursuer. The indian threw his tomahawk which sunk into the back of Mr Job Welton,
2 CONC  severing two ribs. He fell to the ground supposing himself mortally wounded. Mr Job Welton soon recovered for his surprise and proceeded cautiously towards the fort, very weak from blood loss. Mr Delay and three others taken were
2 CONC  scalped and died. Mr Job Welton was able to reach Fort George where he lay for three months before his wound healed. Of the whole party, only three escaped, four were scalped and two were killed at first surprise. Job Welton was 
2 CONC born in 1743 which would have made him about 13 yrs old when this took place. He died in 1820 after having served as Lieutenant in the American Revolution
