2 CONT David Welton ( 1749-1815/later) m. ca 1772 to Elizabeth Stump
2 CONT
2 CONT David was probably born in Virginia. He was named as an heir in his father's will (1764). His marriage to Elizabeth probably occured in Hampshire County. She was the daughter of Catherine and Michael Stump. 19 In 1773 David was gr
2 CONC anted 200 acres on the South Branch Potomac from Felix Seymour, and six years later there was a grant to 102 acres on the South Branch Potomac from Michael Alt. The 255 acres on Looney's Creek, which David received from John Welto
2 CONC n (the son) in 1780 can be traced back to a grant to their father ( apparently before the formation of Hampshire County). 20 He was included among Judy's "Heroes in the Revolution." A taxpayer in Hampshire in the early 1780's and 
2 CONC in Hardy after its partition. David's 1790 personal property taxes included taxes on 18 horses. In 1787 he was a Justice of the county court. 21 He was named in the will of Conrad Moore (founder of Moorefield) and David's own will
2 CONC  was recorded in hardy County in 1815. 22
2 CONT
2 CONT children from David's will: Catherine Welton ( b. 1773) d.y.? Felix Welton (1775-1816/later) m. to Elizabeth Job Welton (b. 1777) m. ca 1801 to ____ Elizabeth Welton ( b. 1780) m. to ___ Rennick Rachel Welton (b. 1782) Catherine W
2 CONC elton (b. 1784) Solomon Welton (b. 1787) Isaac Welton (b. 1789) Rebecca Welton (b. 1789)m. to ____Moducett Jemima Welton (b. 1791) Jesse Welton (b. 1794) Parris Welton (b. 1796)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: 19. Vital dates for David and information on his children from Edith Seagraves of Jennings, Louisiana, citing "family Bibles and Courthouse records."; Elizabeth Stump's parents from Thurman Stump, Michael Stump of Virgini
2 CONC a, (Parsons, W. VA; McClain Printing), 1975, pp. 80 & 111, although Stump incorrectly identified Elizabeth's husband as Felix. 20. Clara Sage & Laura Jones, Early Records, Hampshire Co, Virginia (Now West Virginia) (Delavan, Wisc.
2 CONC : Delavan Republican Printers & Publishers), 1939,pp. 53, 1, and 50. 21. David as justice of county court from Tyler's Vol 5. 22. David named in will of Conrad Moore from "Heroes in the Revolution" from E. L. Judy, A History of Gr
2 CONC ant and Hardy Counties, West Virginia ( Charleston, WVA: Charleston Printing Co.), 1951, p. 306; David's will abstracted in Judy , p. 314
