2 CONT Job Welton (1744-1820) m. to Mary ____ Job was probably born in Virginia, 12 and he would have been a child when his family came into the South Branch Valley. In his classic history, Samuel Kerscheval wrote of a tragic incident in
2 CONC volving Job and his brother Jonathan during the French and Indian War. The brothers had left Fort George with a small group to attend to chores on the Welton farm and were attacked by Indians. Jonathan was killed and young Job was
2 CONC  tomahawked in the back. Despite his wound, he managed to return to the fort, where he "laid three months" convalescing from his injury. 13 Job survived the Indian was and embarked upon a remarkable career. He was a landowner on P
2 CONC atterson Creek as early as 1762 14, and his landholdings, mostly on the South Branch Potomac, steadily increased in subsequent years. He was named as an heir in his father's will (1764). Included among Judy's " Heroes in the Revol
2 CONC ution," Job was listed on the payroll (1775) of Capt. John Harness and achieved the rank of lieutenant rather early in the Revolutionary War. Following the war, he was among the group of justices sworn in as the first court of new
2 CONC ly formed Hardy County. He and Isaac Van Meter were also Hardy's first delegates to the Virginia General Assembly in 1786-87. 15 His prestige and popularity were apparently enhanced by this venture into government service in Virgi
2 CONC nia's capital. When he returned to Hardy County, Job exanded his landholdings with grants in 1788 to land on Looney's Creek and on Stoney River and in 1789 to a property on Allegheny Mountain. 16 A taxpayer in Hampshire County in 
2 CONC the early 1780's and in Hardy after its partition, his 1790 personal property taxes included taxes on 4 slaves and 12 horses. Job apparently continued his involvement with the county militia even after the Revolution. He was contr
2 CONC acted to transort munitions ( arms, powder, flint and lead) to three neighboring counties. It was recorded that the powder was sent in insufficient barrels to cross the mountains and his waggoners lost much of the shipment; "the l
2 CONC oss of lead cannot be accounted for." Even with these difficulties in executing his mission, Welton rose in the ranks to colonel. 17 Job Welton's will was recorded in Hardy county, named the children listed below. 18 He died and w
2 CONC as presumably buried in what is now Grant Co., West Virginia. children from will: John Welton, Aaron Welton, Job Welton, Elizabeth Welton m. 1804 to John Clark, Nancy Welton m. to Peter Hutton
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: 12. Vital dates for Job from Edith Seagraves, of Jennings, Louisiana, citing "family Bibles and Courthouse Records; name of his wife from abstract of Job's will in E. L. Judy, "Heroes in the Revolution" , a History of Gra
2 CONC nt and Hardy Counties, West Virginia (Charleston, WVA; Charleston Printing Co.), 1951, p. 317. 13. Samuel Kerscheval, A History of the Valley of Virginia (Woodstock, Va. : John Gatewood), 1850. 14. Edgar Sims (ed.), Sims Index to 
2 CONC Land Grants in West Virginia ( Charleston, W VA: State of West Virginia), 1952, p. 238. 15. Judy, op cit, pp. 233 & 220; Job in first Hardy County Court and in General Assembly from Judy, pp. 168 & 155. 16. Sim, op. cit., p. 269. 
2 CONC 17. William P. Palmer, M. D. and Sherwin McRae (eds.), Calendar of Virginia State Papers and Other Manuscripts, Vol V (Richmond, VA). 1885, p. 60. 18. Job's children named in his will, abstracted in Judy, op. cit. The will provide
2 CONC d the names of Job's daughters Elizabeth and Nancy; also named in the will is one Moses Welton who might have been the child of a deceased son. The marriage date of Elizabeth and John Clark is from Judy, p. 80
