1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elizabeth Waldegrave, Countess Waldegrave, was a British courtier and noblewoman who served as a Lady of the Bedchamber to Princess Mary. She was born on March 25, 1758, the eldest daughter of John Waldegrave, 3rd 
2 CONC Earl Waldegrave, and Lady Elizabeth Leveson-Gower. Her maternal grandfather was John Leveson-Gower, 1st Earl Gower. She was part of a prominent family that included her brothers George Waldegrave, 4th Earl Waldegrave, and Admiral 
2 CONC William Waldegrave, 1st Baron Radstock. She was raised within the high social circles of the British aristocracy and was a great-niece of the writer Horace Walpole, with whom she maintained a frequent correspondence.
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 5, 1782, Elizabeth married her first cousin, George Waldegrave, Viscount Chewton, at Gloucester House, Grosvenor Square, London. Her husband succeeded his father as the 4th Earl Waldegrave in 1784, at which point she became
2 CONC  the Countess Waldegrave. The marriage produced five children: Lady Maria Wilhelmina, George (who became the 5th Earl), John (who became the 6th Earl), Edward, and William. The union was noted for its close family ties, though it 
2 CONC was relatively brief due to the Earl’s early death in 1789. Following his passing, the Countess was left to manage the upbringing of her young children and the family’s social standing during her long widowhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth held a significant position at the royal court, serving as a Lady of the Bedchamber to Princess Mary, the daughter of King George III. Her role involved attending to the princess during official duties and within the roy
2 CONC al household, a position she maintained for many years. She was also one of the three subjects of Sir Joshua Reynolds's celebrated 1780 oil painting, The Ladies Waldegrave, in which she was depicted alongside her sisters, Charlott
2 CONC e and Anna Horatia. The painting was commissioned by their great-uncle Horace Walpole to showcase the beauty and grace of the sisters to potential suitors. Throughout her life, she remained a central figure in the documentation of
2 CONC  the Waldegrave family's history and the preservation of their artistic legacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Waldegrave died on January 29, 1816, at the age of 57, at Strawberry Hill in Twickenham. She was buried in the family vault at Navestoke, Essex. Her death marked the conclusion of a life spent within the inner circles of
2 CONC  the British court and the landed gentry. Her descendants continued the Waldegrave line, with her sons George and John successively holding the earldom. Her genealogical records and personal correspondence remain primary sources f
2 CONC or historians studying the social and courtly life of the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_Waldegrave,_Countess_Waldegrave
